    "title": "ASCERTAIN Final Conference - ASCERTAIN",
    "text": "ASCERTAIN Final Conference - ASCERTAIN\nASCERTAIN Project\nAbout\nImpact\nWork Packages\nConsortium\nAdvisory Board\nFinal Conference\nNews & Events\nFor Patients\nResults\nContact\nEVENTS\n21. October 2026\nASCERTAIN Final Conference\nFrom Evidence to Access: Rethinking Healthcare Decision-Making in Europe\nAs the ASCERTAIN project comes to a close, we are pleased to invite you to our Final Conference. The event will bring together policymakers, HTA bodies, payers, patient representatives, industry, researchers, and healthcare professionals to explore new approaches to improving access to innovative health technologies in Europe.\nThroughout the day, participants will discover ASCERTAIN’s novel pricing, cost-effectiveness, and reimbursement models, developed to support more transparent, evidence-based, and sustainable healthcare decision-making. These approaches will be showcased through the project’s three healthcare use cases – personalised cancer medicines, CAR T-cell therapies, and Next Generation Sequencing (NGS) tests for precision oncology – as well as the open-access Policy Support Tool that integrates these models into a practical decision-support environment.\nHeld at The Dominican in Brussels, the conference will provide a forum for dialogue, collaboration, and reflection on the future of equitable access, affordability, and healthcare innovation in Europe. Attendance at the conference is free of charge.\nWho should join?\nThe conference is aimed at payers and insurance organisations, policymakers and regulators, industry representatives, patient representatives, researchers and academics, and clinicians interested in the future of healthcare decision-making and sustainable access to innovation in Europe.\nWhy attend?\nExplore ASCERTAIN’s key findings, policy recommendations, and open-access tools, including the ASCERTAIN Policy Support Tool. Join experts and stakeholders to discuss the future of affordability, access, and sustainability in European healthcare systems.\nPayers & Insurance: Explore how ASCERTAIN’s pricing, reimbursement, and cost-effectiveness models can support sustainable healthcare decision-making and budget impact assessments. Gain practical insights into affordability, negotiation processes, and value assessment for innovative health technologies.\nPolicy & Regulation: Learn how ASCERTAIN’s tools can support more evidence-based, transparent, and sustainable healthcare policy-making across Europe. Discuss future approaches to affordability, patient access, and European cooperation in healthcare decision-making.\nIndustry: Gain insights into pricing, reimbursement, and evidence-generation models that may support more transparent and predictable market access pathways. Explore how negotiation parameters, uncertainty, and stakeholder expectations influence future access decisions.\nPatient Representatives: Join discussions on equitable access, affordability, transparency, and patient-centered healthcare decision-making. Explore how ASCERTAIN aims to improve fairness and sustainability in access to innovative health technologies.\nClinicians: Discuss the challenges surrounding access to innovative therapies, reimbursement, and sustainable healthcare systems. Explore future approaches to balancing innovation, affordability, and clinical value in Europe.\nResearch & Academia: Exchange perspectives on innovative methodologies, health technology assessment, pricing, reimbursement, and evidence generation. Explore ASCERTAIN’s integrated framework and discuss future research and policy applications.\nProgramme\nThe programme includes keynote presentations, stakeholder panels, interactive workshops, and hands-on demonstrations of the ASCERTAIN Policy Support Tool. Dedicated breakout sessions and interactive activities will explore future policy pathways, reimbursement models, stakeholder perspectives, and equitable access to innovation. Networking opportunities throughout the day will encourage dialogue and collaboration across stakeholder groups. Please note that the agenda is preliminary and will be continuously updated as the programme develops.\n19:00 – 21:00 | Pre-conference dinner (optional)\nWe invite you to our pre-conference dinner at The Dominican on 20 October 2026.\n08:30 – 09:00 | Registration & Welcome Coffee\nParticipant registration\nInformal networking and arrival coffee\n09:00 – 10:30 | Opening Session: Setting the Scene\n9:00-9:10 Welcome and event introduction // Prof. Carin Uyl-de Groot, Erasmus University Rotterdam\n9:10-9:30 Keynote on affordability, access, and the evolving European HTA and policy landscape // Prof. Dr. rer. pol. Frank-Ulrich Fricke, Technische Hochschule Nürnberg Georg Simon Ohm\n9:30 – 9:55 Introduction to ASCERTAIN’s framework and its novel pricing, cost-effectiveness, and reimbursement models for innovative health technologies // Prof. Carin Uyl-de Groot, Erasmus University Rotterdam; Prof. Eline Aas, University of Oslo; Dr. Maximilian Salcher-Konrad, Gesundheit Österreich\n9:55 – 10:30 Stakeholder panel: perspectives from patients, policymakers, payers, and industry // Prof. Elizabeth MacIntyre, AP-HP ; James Ryan, AstraZeneca; Prof. Zoltán Kaló, National Health Insurance Fund, Hungary; Steven Hartjes, Hematon\n10:30 – 11:00 | Coffee Break & Networking\nRefreshments and informal discussions\n11:00 – 12:00 | ASCERTAIN Policy Support Tool: Models in Practice\nDemonstration of the Policy Support Tool integrating pricing, cost-effectiveness, and reimbursement models\nApplication in three clinical use cases: Personalised cancer medicines, CAR T-cell therapies and Next Generation Sequencing (NGS) tests for precision oncology\nPersona-based walkthroughs using real healthcare decision-making scenarios\nDr. Frederick Thielen, Erasmus University Rotterdam;\nAnne Hendrickx, Association Internationale de la Mutualité;\nPia Henkel, University of Oslo;\nKine Pedersen, University of Oslo;\nJosephine Mosset, Cancer Patients Europe;\nDominik Griebling, Fresenius;\nSahar Van Waalwijk van Doorn-Khosrovani, CZ Health Insurance\n12:00 – 12:15 | Let's recharge together.\n12:15 – 13:00 | Interactive ASCERTAIN Experience Hub\nOne-on-one demonstrations of the Policy Support Tool with consortium experts\n“Meet the expert” discussion area\nFuture of Healthcare Wall: How can Europe ensure sustainable and equitable access to\ninnovative health technologies over the next decade?\n13:00 – 14:00 | Lunch Break\nNetworking lunch\n14:00 – 15:30 | Parallel Stakeholder Workshops\nApplying ASCERTAIN’s pricing, cost-effectiveness, and reimbursement models to real-world decision-making scenarios\nAccess-based Pricing Lab (Anne Hendrickx, Association Internationale de la Mutualité; Maximilian Salcher-Konrad, Gesundheit Österreich; Nicolas Xander, Erasmus University Rotterdam)\nCost-effectiveness and Budget Impact Lab (Prof. Eline Aas, University of Oslo; Pia Henkel, University of Oslo; Kine Pedersen, University of Oslo; Yancy S. Wu, University of Oslo)\nCost-effectiveness Thresholds Lab (Prof. Carin Uyl-de Groot, Erasmus University Rotterdam; Tom Belleman, Erasmus University Rotterdam)\n15:30 – 15:45 | Coffee Break\nRefreshments and networking\n15:45 – 16:30 | Final Stakeholder Reflection Panel\nReflections on ASCERTAIN’s pricing, cost-effectiveness and reimbursement models,\nPolicy Support Tool, and their potential for real-world adoption\nDiscussion on adoption opportunities and implementation barriers\nStakeholder perspectives on future relevance\nRobin Doeswijk, European Hematology Association;\nProf. Wim van Harten, Netherlands Cancer Institute;\nDr. Joanne Hackett, IQVIA;\nCharlotte Roffiaen, France Assos Santé;\nSara Marques\n16:30 – 16:45 | Closing Remarks & Future Outlook\nConsortium vision beyond the project\nSustainability, partnerships, and future opportunities\nNext steps for the ASCERTAIN ecosystem\nProf. Carin Uyl-de Groot, Erasmus University\n16:45 – 17:30 | Reception Drinks & Networking\nInformal closing reception\nContinued stakeholder exchange and networking\nSpeakers\nCarin Uyl-de Groot\nProf. Dr. Carin Uyl-de Groot is Professor of Health Technology Assessment at Erasmus University Rotterdam and Director of the institute for Medical Technology Assessment (iMTA) in the Netherlands. She is the project lead of ASCERTAIN and has extensive experience in health technology assessment, health economics, and cancer research. Her work focuses on cost-effectiveness, outcomes research, pricing, reimbursement, and implementation studies, particularly in oncology. She has served in leading roles within international organisations and conferences, including ISPOR and ESMO, and has been a member of the Dutch Health Council since 2015. In addition, she has contributed to the development of clinical practice guidelines and leads several cancer registries and outcomes studies.\nAnne Hendrickx\nAnne Hendrickx holds a Master in Economics from the Université Libre de Bruxelles. After nine years in humanitarian non-governmental organisations, she became an advisor on drug policy in the cabinet of the Minister of Social Affairs and Public Health in 2003. Since 2015, she has been an advisor in the Research Department of the Belgian Mutual Fund Solidaris, focusing on national and european issues relating to medicines. She is a member of the Committee for Reimbursement of Medicines of the Belgian National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance and of AIM’s Working Group on pharmaceuticals and medical devices, where she leads the “European model for fair prices of medicines” project.\nBenjamin P. Geisler\nBenjamin P. Geisler is a German-American board-certified internist who earned his medical degree at Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin. After a decision-sciences research fellowship in Boston, Ben completed an MPH at Harvard and an internal medicine residency at NYU School of Medicine. He then worked as an attending at Massachusetts General Hospital, instructor at Harvard Medical School, and senior consultant in early HTA for medical device companies. Passionate about clinically relevant outcomes research, Ben has also worked in research groups in Adelaide, Australia and Munich, Germany. In 2022, he relocated to Oslo, Norway where he is currently a doctoral research fellow in health economics.\nCharlotte Roffiaen\nCharlotte Roffiaen is a Sciences Po Paris graduate and trained lawyer specialised in EU law. She has been involved in health advocacy since 2001 and has advised France Assos Santé on its European strategy since 2010, focusing on access to medicines. France Assos Santé represents around 100 healthcare users’ organisations and is an active member of the European Patients’ Forum (EPF). Charlotte has also represented patients and consumers at the European Medicines Agency within the Executive Steering Group on Shortages and Safety of Medicinal Products (MSSG). Previously, she managed two European organisations: Active Citizenship Network and Lymphoma Coalition Europe.\nDominik Griebling\nDominik Griebling is Senior Manager Scientific Strategy & Project Coordination at Fresenius SE & Co. KGaA, Germany. He holds an MSc in Biomedicine from the University of Würzburg and has worked across Fresenius at the interface of science, strategy and business for nearly seven years. He coordinates the IHI-funded EU project EASYGEN, developing an automated platform for decentralised, point-of-care manufacturing of CAR T-cell therapies, and co-leads its work on next-generation CAR-T gene editing technologies. He contributes the EASYGEN perspective on improving access to CAR T-cell therapies and is co-first author of a 2026 Cytotherapy perspective addressing access barriers.\nEline Aas\nProf. Dr. Eline Aas is Professor at the Department of Health Management and Health Economics at the University of Oslo and holds a joint position at the Norwegian Institute of Public Health. Her research focuses on economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, decision-analytic modelling, register data analysis, health behaviour, and health inequalities. She has evaluated clinical trials across areas including colorectal cancer screening, precision cancer medicine, liver transplantation, hip fractures, traumatic brain injury rehabilitation, and mental health. Many studies combine clinical trial and register data. Prof. Aas leads several research projects and teaches master’s courses in Economic Evaluation, including the EU-HEM programme.\nElizabeth Macintyre\nElizabeth Macintyre is Franco-Scottish and trained in Internal Medicine/Hematology in England, completed her PhD in Paris and her Post-Doctoral studies at Harvard. Professor of Hematology at Université Paris Cité since 1992, she headed Diagnostic Hematology at Necker–Enfants Malades Hospital from 1999–2018 and co-directs an INSERM team on lymphoid malignancies. She was President of the European Hematology Association from 2021–23 and of the Biomedical Alliance in Europe from 2024–25. She is currently a stakeholder in the European Commission Medical Devices Coordination Group and has recently joined the European School of Hematology executive board.\nFrank-Ulrich Fricke\nProf. Dr. Frank-Ulrich Fricke is Professor of Health Economics at Technische Hochschule Nürnberg Georg Simon Ohm. He studied business administration at the Universities of Bayreuth and Kiel and worked as a research associate in economics, with a focus on health economics, at the University of Bayreuth. His professional experience includes positions at Medical Relations, Novartis Pharma Germany, Fricke & Pirk, and IMS Health Economics & Outcomes Research. From 2011 to 2015, he served as Deputy Member of the German Arbitration Board on Drug Prices and has been a full member since July 2015. He served as Faculty Dean from 2017 to 2026.\nFrederick Thielen\nFrederick Thielen is Assistant Professor at the Erasmus School of Health Policy and Management, Erasmus University Rotterdam, and Senior Scientific Researcher at the Trimbos Institute in Utrecht. His research focuses on health economics and outcomes studies. He has extensive experience in international project collaborations and is involved in several research consortia, including European Horizon projects. A nurse by background, he worked at the internal medicine ward of University Hospital Aachen. He studied European Public Health and Health Sciences at Maastricht University and obtained his PhD from Erasmus University Rotterdam in 2020, focusing on challenges in health technology assessment and haemato-oncology.\nJames Ryan\nJames Ryan is Senior Director, Global HTA Policy at AstraZeneca, leading initiatives in health technology assessment, payer evidence, health economic modelling, and policy development. He is an industry expert in the implementation of the EU HTA Regulation and Joint Clinical Assessments (JCA) and leads AstraZeneca’s internal JCA readiness. James serves as Vice-Chair of the EFPIA HTA Working Group and represents AstraZeneca at the HTAi Global Policy Forum. He has contributed to numerous industry, academic, and policy initiatives focused on HTA and value assessment. He holds an MSc in Health Economics from the University of York and a BA in Economics.\nJosephine Mosset\nJosephine Mosset is Head of Policy at Cancer Patients Europe (CPE), where she leads the organisation’s policy and advocacy work at EU level. She specialises in EU health policy, patient advocacy, health technology assessment and access to innovation, with a focus on ensuring patient perspectives are reflected in health policy and decision-making. Before joining CPE in 2024, Josephine held roles across the nonprofit and private sectors, including at EURORDIS, Friends of Europe, the Africa-Europe Foundation and Amgen. She holds a master’s in public policy, specialising in Global Health, from Sciences Po Paris.\nJoanne M. Hackett\nDr. Joanne M. Hackett is Vice President and Global Head of Health System Services at IQVIA, leading global strategy and innovation across healthcare systems, data, and transformation. She previously served as Chief Commercial Officer at Genomics England. A clinical academic, entrepreneur, and investor, she has extensive experience spanning start-ups, public institutions, and Fortune 500 organisations. Her work focuses on personalised medicine, prevention, open science, citizen genomics, and scalable, cost-effective healthcare solutions. An internationally recognised healthcare leader, she has received numerous awards for innovation and leadership. She is also a sought-after speaker and internationally known yoga instructor, promoting the belief that health equals wealth.\nMaximilian Salcher-Konrad\nMaximilian Salcher-Konrad is a Senior Health Expert and Deputy Head of the Pharmacoeconomics Department at Gesundheit Österreich GmbH, the Austrian National Public Health Institute. His research focuses on pharmaceutical pricing, reimbursement, procurement, and policy. He holds an MSc in Health Policy, Planning and Financing from the London School of Economics and the London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine, and a BSc in Economics from the Vienna University of Economics and Business. Previously a Research Fellow at the LSE, he has led and published studies, systematic reviews, and meta-analyses on comparative effectiveness, pharmaceutical regulation, and economic evaluation.\nNicolas Xander\nNicolas Xander is a PhD candidate at the department of Health Technology Assessment at the Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management, Erasmus University Rotterdam. Originally, he has a background in law (diploma 2012, doctorate 2018), and acquired working experience in various law offices in Liechtenstein as well as in the Liechtenstein state administration. He obtained a master’s degree in Health Economics, Policy & Law at Erasmus University in 2022. His research concerns the pricing, economic evaluation, and reimbursement decision-making of anti-cancer treatments.\nPia Henkel\nPia Sofie Henkel is a Doctoral Research Fellow at the Department of Health Management and Health Economics at the University of Oslo. Her research focuses on health economic evaluation and modelling, next-generation sequencing for precision cancer medicine, and integrating environmental aspects into economic assessments. She is also interested in European HTA processes and improving market access to innovative health technologies. Pia holds a European Master in Health Economics and Management (EU-HEM, 2022) and a Bachelor of Arts in International Business (2018) from accadis Hochschule Bad Homburg, Germany, providing a strong foundation for her research in health economics.\nRobin Doeswijk\nRobin Doeswijk is Head of European Affairs at the European Hematology Association (EHA), representing hematology professionals across Europe and beyond. With a background in International Relations and public affairs, he oversees EHA’s policy, advocacy and regulatory work, partnerships and alliances. EHA’s policy and regulatory work focuses on improving affordable and equal access to innovative therapies, with particular attention to EU Health Technology Assessment and the cost-benefit assessment and pricing of new medicines. He chairs the BioMed Alliance HTA Working Group and is a member of the EMA HCP Working Party, as well as a faculty member of the European Access Academy.\nSahar Van Waalwijk van Doorn-Khosrovani\nSara Marques\nSara Marques is a life sciences leader with 15 years of experience across the pharmaceutical industry, health policy consulting and academia. A pharmacist with a PhD in oncology, she has worked at the intersection of health policy and commercial strategy across Europe and Canada. Most recently at AstraZeneca, Sara held senior regional roles spanning oncology policy, government affairs, commercial strategy and launch readiness. She led policy and health system initiatives and cross-country strategies for innovative oncology medicines. Sara is particularly interested in bringing diverse stakeholder perspectives together to address health system challenges and support sustainable patient access to innovation across Europe.\nStavros Anagnostopoulos\nStavros Anagnostopoulos, MSc, is a researcher at the Institute for Medical Technology Assessment (iMTA). He holds a Pharmacy degree from the Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (2021), and a master’s degree in Health Economics, Policy & Law from Erasmus University Rotterdam (2022), with his master’s thesis assessing the effects informal care on carers’ mental health in the Dutch setting. Since joining iMTA in 2023, Stavros has been involved in various health economics research projects, with a focus on health technology assessments, including cost-effectiveness modelling and systematic reviews across a range of disease areas and Treatments.\nSteven Hartjes\nSteven Hartjes is a patient advocate at Hematon, the Dutch patient organisation for leukemia, lymphoma and myeloma. A former patient, he joined Hematon in 2021. He has a background in business economics and spent his 40-year career at EY Auditors, including extensive work in data analytics. He is currently an unpaid guest researcher at Leiden University Medical Center, focusing on generating Real World Evidence using healthcare insurance data. He represents Hematon in the global Lymphoma Coalition, serves on the Steering Committee of RWD4decisions as a patient advocate, and is a member of the Lymphoma Coalition’s HTA special interest group.\nYancy S Wu\nYancy S Wu is a Doctoral Research Fellow at the Department of Health Management and Health Economics at the University of Oslo. His research applies mathematical modelling to evaluate the cost, efficacy, and cost-effectiveness of pharmaceuticals, medical devices, and healthcare policies, particularly in oncology and disease prevention. He is involved in the EU-funded ASCERTAIN project, developing cost-effectiveness and budget impact models for innovative health technologies. Yancy holds an M.Phil. in Health Economics, Policy, and Management from the University of Oslo and a B.A. in Economics from Boston University. In 2023, he received the Lee B. Lusted Student Award.\nZoltán Kaló\nProf. Zoltán Kaló is Director General of the Hungarian National Health Insurance Fund and Professor of Health Economics at the Center for Health Technology Assessment at Semmelweis University in Budapest. For 18 years, he was the lead partner of Syreon Research Institute. He has over 30 years of international experience in academia and industry, specialising in health systems design, healthcare financing, health technology assessment, patient access and pricing policies. During his career, Dr. Kaló has served as a policy advisor to public decision-makers, global healthcare corporations and scientific societies. In 2021, he received the ISPOR Marilyn Dix Smith Leadership Award.\nEvent location\nThe Dominican\nRue Léopold / Leopoldstraat 9\n1000 Brussels, Belgium\nVisit the hotel website here.\nPhoto: thedominican.be\nBooking\nThe registration for the conference is now closed.\nBuchungen sind für diese Veranstaltung nicht mehr möglich.\nOverview\nSUPPORT\nLegal Notice\nPrivacy Policy\nCookie Policy\nSITEMAP\nASCERTAIN Project\nAbout\nImpact\nWork Packages\nConsortium\nAdvisory Board\nFinal Conference\nNews & Events\nFor Patients\nResults\nContact\nStay up to date!\nTwice a year we will be sending out regular newsletters on the status of our project.\nEmail\nLast name\nFirst name\nI have read the privacy policy and I want to receive the Newsletter.\nSend\nWe use an external service provider to send our newsletter. You can find more information in our privacy policy.\nThank you for your subscription!\nWe have sent you an email - please confirm your email address by clicking the activation link in it.\nWe‘ll send only nice news, don‘t worry.\nFollow\nFollow\nFollow\n© 2026: OptiMedis AG\nViews and opinions expressed are however those of the author(